.content.0.sections.0.body.1.text :: So anything that changes the demand for the final product changes the demand for the people who make it. In Tellmar, export orders for shirts rise. Workshops need more hours of sewing to fill them, so at every wage they want more 
.content.0.sections.0.body.2.steps.1.title :: Workshops need more output
.content.0.sections.0.recall.why.2 :: More output needs more workers at any given wage.
.content.0.sections.1.body.1.text :: What the firm cares about is what that extra output adds to its revenue. Arun Shirts is one of many workshops and sells every extra shirt at the market price of $3, so that is the extra shirts times the price. Textbooks call it th
.content.0.sections.2.body.0.text :: For a firm that sells at a given price, as Arun Shirts does, what a worker adds is worth two things multiplied together: how much the worker produces, and what each unit sells for. Change either, and the firm's demand for labour *
.content.0.sections.3.body.3.text :: So a fall in the price of capital can shift the demand for labour left with wages unchanged. It is not always so: a machine that needs an operator, or that makes each worker more productive, can raise the demand for the people who
.content.0.sections.3.realExample.text :: Supermarkets from Singapore to Nairobi have installed self-checkout tills as the machines became cheaper. Each till does part of a cashier's work, so fewer cashiers are needed at each wage.
.content.0.sections.4.realExample.text :: A pay rise for airline pilots barely changes how many an airline employs: every flight needs its crew, and their pay is a modest share of costs next to fuel and aircraft. The same rise for car-park attendants hastens the switch to
.content.0.sections.4.examMatters :: Appendix 6 gives Explain 4 marks. Each factor needs its reason: not "substitutes", but "a machine can do the work, so firms switch when wages rise".
.content.1.sections.0.misconception :: Students shift the supply curve right when the wage rises. The wage is on the vertical axis, so a higher wage is a movement along the curve to a larger quantity supplied. A shift needs one of the six factors.
.content.1.sections.3.body.1.items.0 :: **Licensing and qualifications.** Doctors, pilots and electricians need a licence, and a new requirement removes everyone without it.
.content.1.sections.3.realExample.text :: Singapore's work-pass system sets the pay and qualifications a foreign worker needs for each kind of pass, so it shapes the supply of labour to jobs from construction to finance.
.content.1.sections.4.keyIdea :: The elasticity of supply of labour measures how strongly the number of workers responds to a change in the wage. It is low where a job needs long training or rare skills.
.content.1.sections.4.realExample.text :: When hospitals need more specialist doctors, pay rises well before numbers do: a specialist takes many years to train, so for a long time the extra pay mostly goes to the specialists already working.
.content.1.sections.4.misconception :: Students write that inelastic supply means workers do not care about pay. It means they cannot respond quickly: the qualifications, skills or location the job needs take time to acquire.
.content.1.sections.4.examMatters :: Appendix 6 gives Explain 4 marks, so each factor needs its reason: "supply is inelastic because training takes years, so pay can rise without new entrants for a long time" is both stages.
.content.1.sections.4.recall.groups.1.why :: Each needs years of training and a licence, so few new workers can appear however much pay rises.
.content.3.sections.0.body.1.text :: Some unions insist on long apprenticeships, set the qualifications a newcomer needs, or agree with employers that only members will be hired. Each reduces how many people can offer themselves for the job at every wage.
.content.4.sections.0.examMatters :: 3.3.4 · 4a asks for causes AND consequences. Housing, family and information are the causes; regional unemployment, unfilled vacancies and a lasting wage gap are the consequences, and an answer needs both.
.content.4.sections.1.body.3.steps.1.title :: Workers lack the skills new jobs need
.content.4.sections.1.realExample.text :: As garment factories close in one region, their machinists cannot simply become the nurses, electricians or software testers that growing sectors are short of: each of those needs a qualification that takes years.
.content.4.sections.1.examMatters :: Appendix 6 says a Discuss needs a critical assessment. Here it is how long the immobility lasts: some workers retrain within a year, while older workers with narrow skills may never move.
.content.4.sections.2.examMatters :: An Evaluate on labour market failure needs a justified judgement (Appendix 6). A strong one weighs how large and how lasting the immobility is: a gap that closes within a year is a friction; one that lasts a decade is a serious fa
.content.4.takeaway.1 :: Occupational immobility: the job exists, but needs other skills.
.notes.0.blocks.1.items.0.text :: Hiring rule: hire while what the extra worker adds to revenue ≥ wage. For a firm selling at a given price, that is extra output × price; in general, extra output × marginal revenue. Arun Shirts: at $12 an hour, 6 machinists; at $1
.quiz.3.explanation :: Each worker's output now sells for more, so more workers are worth hiring at every wage: the whole demand curve moves right. A movement along the curve needs a change in the wage, and the supply of labour is unaffected by what the
.quiz.5.question :: A worker adds 5 units an hour, and the firm sells every unit at the market price of $4. The worker's extra output is worth, per hour:
.quiz.5.explanation :: The value of the extra output is the extra units times the price: 5 × $4 = $20. Adding the two figures, or dividing one by the other, has no meaning here.
.quiz.10.explanation :: The wage is on the vertical axis, so a change in it moves along the curves rather than shifting them: more drivers offer themselves at the higher wage. Shifts need a cause other than the wage.
.quiz.11.options.0 :: the job needs years of training
.quiz.11.options.1 :: the skills needed are widely held
.quiz.23.explanation :: Public-sector pay is an administrative decision, taken through pay scales, pay commissions and bargaining with public-sector unions, with the government budget as the limit. There is no competitive market price for most public-sec
.practice.2.guidance :: Two factors, each with its reason. Choose factors you can tie to how quickly extra workers can appear when pay rises, and say which way each pushes the elasticity. Each factor earns a mark for identifying it and a mark for explain
.practice.3.guidance :: Follow one chain through linked stages, and use a diagram. Keep the demand curve where it is unless you give a reason to move it, and say which curve shifts before saying what happens to the wage. Knowledge: net migration is immig
.flashcards.3.back :: Hire while what the extra worker adds to revenue is at least the wage. For a firm selling at a given price, that is the extra output times the price of the product.
.flashcards.4.front :: What is the marginal revenue product (MRP)?
.flashcards.4.back :: The standard name for what one more worker adds to the firm's revenue: extra output × marginal revenue. Only for a firm selling at a given price does that equal extra output × the price of the product.
.mistakes.2.title :: Valuing extra output at the price when the firm must cut its price to sell more
